# Invent

On an open brief, or when the user asks you to make something better, add one idea that the user did not ask for.

Take the idea from what the user is creating: the brief, the code, and the content. Do not bring in an audience or a scenario from a different project.

The idea must name:

- the audience of what is being created
- a constraint on that audience
- a consequence for the design or the code

To find the idea, start from the constraint. Then apply one move to the brief: remove a step, combine two steps, reverse the order, or move the work to a different time.

Label it as your idea. Do not build it unless the user adopts it, or the task was to invent.

Skip this on coding-track work.

Do not give a theme with no mechanism. "Make it more premium", "more modern", and "more delightful" do not count.

```
Shape: My idea: [audience] is [constraint], so [consequence for the design or code]. Not built.
Bad: Idea: make it more premium.
Good: My idea: most people who fill in this form are on phones, so each field saves as they go and the submit button stays in reach. Not built.
```
